1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:23,880
This is Doing and Feeling, the long version. Episode 5, The Predictive Brain. Take a hollow mask of a face and turn it so you're looking at its inside, the concave side. You'd expect to see a hollow. Most people do not. They see a normal face bulging toward them, and if the mask rotates, the face seems to turn the wrong way, eerily tracking them. Knowing that it's hollow does not help.

2
00:00:23,880 --> 00:00:28,520
Your brain has an extremely strong expectation that faces are convex,

3
00:00:28,660 --> 00:00:33,460
and the light reaching your eyes is ambiguous enough that the expectation wins.

4
00:00:33,660 --> 00:00:39,440
In one small brain imaging study from 2009, with 13 patients and 16 controls,

5
00:00:40,040 --> 00:00:42,820
healthy volunteers were fooled almost every time,

6
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:46,340
while people with schizophrenia were fooled far less often.

7
00:00:46,660 --> 00:00:52,840
It's a small sample, but it fits the idea that the balance between expectation and evidence is a setting,

8
00:00:53,880 --> 00:00:59,020
be set differently. This episode argues that the brain is well described as a prediction machine,

9
00:00:59,220 --> 00:01:03,320
and then shows where that description is solid and where it is still mostly slogan.

10
00:01:03,580 --> 00:01:08,680
We'll put the idea through five tests. Perception, the wiring of the cortex,

11
00:01:09,140 --> 00:01:15,220
a grand unifying principle, reward, and the brain's internal maps. It's the bridge between

12
00:01:15,220 --> 00:01:19,080
the computational story of the last episode and the machines of the next.

13
00:01:19,980 --> 00:01:25,040
The modern form of the idea starts with Hermann von Helmholtz in the 1860s.

14
00:01:25,420 --> 00:01:29,120
Perception, he argued, involves unconscious inference.

15
00:01:29,440 --> 00:01:35,600
The brain receives ambiguous signals and infers their most probable causes, drawing on experience.

16
00:01:36,020 --> 00:01:38,060
The modern form is Bayesian.

17
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:43,260
Combine a likelihood, how well each possible cause would explain the incoming signals,

18
00:01:43,320 --> 00:01:46,900
with a prior, how probable each cause was to begin with,

19
00:01:46,900 --> 00:01:49,880
and you get a posterior, the brain's best guess.

20
00:01:50,420 --> 00:01:54,880
The hollow mask is what happens when the prior is strong and the likelihood is weak.

21
00:01:55,120 --> 00:01:56,960
Is the brain actually Bayesian?

22
00:01:57,180 --> 00:02:00,660
The best evidence comes from experiments on combining the senses.

23
00:02:01,020 --> 00:02:07,980
In 2002, Mark Ernst and Martin Banks had people judge the height of a bar they could both see and feel.

24
00:02:08,240 --> 00:02:13,000
When the researchers added noise to the visual display, people leaned more on touch,

25
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:17,640
and they weighted the two senses almost exactly as an ideal statistician would,

26
00:02:17,840 --> 00:02:19,980
in proportion to each one's reliability.

27
00:02:20,260 --> 00:02:24,580
But there is a long list of cases where people are measurably not optimal.

28
00:02:24,920 --> 00:02:30,960
In 2018, Dobromir Ranev and Rachel Denison cataloged them in a Target article

29
00:02:30,960 --> 00:02:33,640
for the journal Behavioral and Brain Sciences,

30
00:02:33,960 --> 00:02:38,840
and argued that claims of optimality are often flexible enough to fit almost anything.

31
00:02:39,080 --> 00:02:42,400
Their advice was to stop asking whether the brain is optimal

32
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:49,400
start building explicit models of what observers actually do. In that journal, a target article is

33
00:02:49,400 --> 00:02:55,060
published together with many short commentaries from other researchers and a reply from the authors,

34
00:02:55,060 --> 00:03:01,980
which is a good way to see how contested even this basic claim is. So is the brain Bayesian or not?

35
00:03:02,260 --> 00:03:08,280
The fairest answer is that it uses priors and weights evidence by reliability, approximately,

36
00:03:08,280 --> 00:03:14,280
with systematic shortcuts. Bayesian is a good framework for asking what the brain is computing.

37
00:03:14,620 --> 00:03:17,740
It is not a finding that the brain computes it perfectly.

38
00:03:18,700 --> 00:03:26,740
The second test is the cortex itself. In 1999, Rajesh Rao and Dana Ballard proposed an influential

39
00:03:26,740 --> 00:03:33,280
model of predictive coding in visual cortex. The visual system is a hierarchy. In their model,

40
00:03:33,620 --> 00:03:37,180
each level sends predictions down about what the level below should be seeing,

41
00:03:38,280 --> 00:03:43,560
only the prediction error, the part of the signal that was not predicted. When predictions are good,

42
00:03:43,900 --> 00:03:49,100
little needs to travel upward. Their model explained puzzling properties of neurons in

43
00:03:49,100 --> 00:03:54,760
visual cortex, such as cells that respond less to a line when it extends beyond the patch they

44
00:03:54,760 --> 00:04:00,300
monitor, because the extension was predictable from context. That's a compression scheme.

45
00:04:00,720 --> 00:04:06,420
Video codecs do the same thing. Send keyframes, then only the differences from what you'd predict.

46
00:04:08,280 --> 00:04:13,640
episode 3's equivalence of prediction and compression, is no accident. How much of what

47
00:04:13,640 --> 00:04:20,600
comes in actually reaches behavior? In a 2024 paper, Ji-Yu Zhang and Marcus Meister estimated

48
00:04:20,600 --> 00:04:26,100
that human behavior carries about 10 bits of information per second, while our senses take

49
00:04:26,100 --> 00:04:30,380
in something like a billion. They called the gap the unbearable slowness of being.

50
00:04:30,600 --> 00:04:34,720
The authors stress how little anyone understands about that bottleneck,

51
00:04:34,720 --> 00:04:38,880
but it is at least consistent with a brain that keeps a model of its world

52
00:04:38,880 --> 00:04:42,300
and passes on mainly what the model failed to anticipate.

53
00:04:42,620 --> 00:04:45,680
The supporting evidence for predictive coding is real.

54
00:04:46,160 --> 00:04:49,080
Neural responses shrink when a stimulus repeats,

55
00:04:49,220 --> 00:04:54,940
and in 2008, Christopher Summerfield and colleagues showed with functional MRI

56
00:04:54,940 --> 00:04:58,480
that this repetition suppression in a face-processing region

57
00:04:58,480 --> 00:05:00,840
was stronger when repeats were expected.

58
00:05:00,840 --> 00:05:04,160
A well-known brainwave, the mismatched negativity,

59
00:05:04,720 --> 00:05:08,500
appears when a run of identical tones is broken by a deviant one.

60
00:05:08,700 --> 00:05:11,600
And if you omit a tone from a predictable sequence,

61
00:05:11,940 --> 00:05:14,380
auditory cortex responds to the silence.

62
00:05:14,700 --> 00:05:17,900
In predictive coding, attention becomes precision.

63
00:05:18,200 --> 00:05:21,420
The weight the brain gives to prediction errors from a source,

64
00:05:21,660 --> 00:05:24,940
depending on how reliable that source is expected to be.

65
00:05:25,340 --> 00:05:26,240
Now the mess.

66
00:05:26,460 --> 00:05:30,880
When Dmitry Kalyakovich and Rufin Vogels recorded single neurons

67
00:05:30,880 --> 00:05:32,800
in the monkey's temporal cortex,

68
00:05:34,720 --> 00:05:40,240
not depend on how likely the repeat was. Summerfield's own group later called the mismatch

69
00:05:40,240 --> 00:05:45,460
between the brain scans and the neurons unexplained. And there's a live dispute right now.

70
00:05:45,980 --> 00:05:52,380
A large preprint led by Jacob Westerberg and Andrei Bastos, recording from Mice and Monkeys,

71
00:05:52,900 --> 00:05:58,000
reported that most oddball responses in sensory cortex are explained by adaptation,

72
00:05:58,440 --> 00:06:04,100
neurons simply tiring of repeated input. Responses to violations of a learned sequence

73
00:06:04,720 --> 00:06:11,400
came late, and in the monkeys, appeared first in prefrontal cortex, which suggests the deep

74
00:06:11,400 --> 00:06:17,360
prediction errors may be computed higher up rather than at every level. Then, in January 2026,

75
00:06:17,980 --> 00:06:24,480
another preprint from Jordan Ham's lab found that primary visual cortex in mice does respond to

76
00:06:24,480 --> 00:06:30,480
sequence-level surprises, learned within fewer than 10 repetitions, and that the effect vanished

77
00:06:30,480 --> 00:06:36,760
when feedback from a frontal region, the anterior cingulate, was silenced. Both were posted as per

78
00:06:36,760 --> 00:06:41,120
prints, and they may be measuring somewhat different things. But notice what is being

79
00:06:41,120 --> 00:06:47,580
argued about. Nobody in this fight doubts that the brain predicts. In 2025, the International

80
00:06:47,580 --> 00:06:53,320
Brain Laboratory published a brain-wide map of mice making decisions, recorded from more than

81
00:06:53,320 --> 00:07:00,560
half a million neurons across 279 brain areas. It found the animal's prior expectations encoded

82
00:07:00,560 --> 00:07:05,580
in at least a fifth to a third of the regions, at every level from early sensory areas,

83
00:07:05,760 --> 00:07:11,720
including the thalamus, up to motor and frontal cortex. What's in question is the specific

84
00:07:11,720 --> 00:07:16,020
textbook circuit, in which every level computes errors and passes them up.

85
00:07:16,920 --> 00:07:20,260
The third test is the grandest version of the idea.

86
00:07:20,660 --> 00:07:26,560
Starting in the mid-2000s, the neuroscientist Carl Fristen proposed the free energy principle.

87
00:07:26,980 --> 00:07:28,400
Here's the core logic.

88
00:07:28,700 --> 00:07:32,660
Any system that persists, a cell, an organism, a brain,

89
00:07:32,860 --> 00:07:36,500
must stay within a limited set of states compatible with its existence.

90
00:07:36,860 --> 00:07:38,740
A fish must stay in water.

91
00:07:39,140 --> 00:07:43,140
In information-theoretic terms, it must avoid surprising states.

92
00:07:43,140 --> 00:07:49,820
But a system cannot measure surprise directly, because that would require knowing the true probability of everything.

93
00:07:50,300 --> 00:07:57,200
What it can do is minimize a quantity called variational free energy, which is always at least as large as surprise.

94
00:07:57,860 --> 00:08:00,720
Keep free energy low, and you keep surprise low.

95
00:08:01,040 --> 00:08:07,160
That's the evidence lower bound from variational inference. Free energy is just its negative.

96
00:08:07,660 --> 00:08:09,540
Exactly the same mathematics.

97
00:08:10,320 --> 00:08:12,200
Minimizing it does two things at once.

98
00:08:12,380 --> 00:08:15,960
It makes the system's approximate posterior closer to the true one,

99
00:08:16,060 --> 00:08:19,280
and it improves how well the system's model fits its data.

100
00:08:19,740 --> 00:08:26,460
In a variational autoencoder, an encoder network learns to produce that approximate posterior in a single pass.

101
00:08:26,900 --> 00:08:31,560
Predictive coding settles on it gradually by passing errors back and forth.

102
00:08:32,180 --> 00:08:33,920
Friston adds two big ideas.

103
00:08:34,320 --> 00:08:39,560
The first is the Markov blanket, a statistical boundary that separates a system's internal

104
00:08:39,560 --> 00:08:44,560
states from the world, mediated by sensory states coming in and active states going out.

105
00:08:44,880 --> 00:08:46,880
The second is active inference.

106
00:08:47,120 --> 00:08:52,540
An organism can reduce prediction error in two ways, change its predictions to fit the

107
00:08:52,540 --> 00:08:57,500
world, which is perception, or change the world to fit its predictions, which is action.

108
00:08:57,740 --> 00:09:02,640
When you reach for a cup, on this view, your brain predicts the sensations of grasping

109
00:09:03,920 --> 00:09:10,000
act to make that prediction come true. To choose actions, agents score them by a related quantity

110
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:14,840
called expected free energy, which combines getting what you prefer with reducing your

111
00:09:14,840 --> 00:09:21,140
uncertainty. That second part is a principled account of curiosity. There's a classic objection

112
00:09:21,140 --> 00:09:27,480
called the dark room problem. If organisms minimize surprise, why do not we all sit in a dark, quiet

113
00:09:27,480 --> 00:09:33,440
room? The standard answer, from a 2012 paper by Friston, Christopher Thornton, and Andy Clark,

114
00:09:33,920 --> 00:09:37,360
is that surprise is relative to an organism's model of itself.

115
00:09:37,700 --> 00:09:41,500
A creature that expects to eat, stay warm and explore

116
00:09:41,500 --> 00:09:43,840
would find a dark room deeply surprising.

117
00:09:44,860 --> 00:09:47,020
The principle has serious critics.

118
00:09:47,400 --> 00:09:51,260
If minimizing free energy covers perception, action,

119
00:09:51,720 --> 00:09:53,620
attention and learning all at once,

120
00:09:53,920 --> 00:09:56,440
what observation could ever count against it?

121
00:09:56,700 --> 00:10:00,820
That's the first criticism, and Friston largely agrees, in a sense.

122
00:10:01,020 --> 00:10:03,540
He describes it as a mathematical principle,

123
00:10:03,920 --> 00:10:08,300
like the principle of least action in physics, from which testable process theories,

124
00:10:08,540 --> 00:10:13,460
such as predictive coding, can be derived. Critics reply that a framework that can describe

125
00:10:13,460 --> 00:10:18,320
anything explains nothing until you commit to a specific process theory, and the process

126
00:10:18,320 --> 00:10:22,900
theories are where the evidence lives, and where, as we just saw, the fights are.

127
00:10:23,080 --> 00:10:29,940
The second is technical. In a 2022 Behavioral and Brain Sciences Target article titled The

128
00:10:29,940 --> 00:10:36,300
Emperor's New Markov Blankets, Gell Bruinberg and colleagues argued that the field uses Markov

129
00:10:36,300 --> 00:10:43,180
blankets in two senses. One is a formal tool from statistics, a property of a model. The other is a

130
00:10:43,180 --> 00:10:49,260
claim about real physical boundaries between organisms and their environments. Sliding between

131
00:10:49,260 --> 00:10:54,820
them, they argue, makes the framework seem to deliver more than it does. The third is a tension

132
00:10:54,820 --> 00:11:01,460
with reinforcement learning, which says agents maximize reward. Friston's reply is that rewards

133
00:11:01,460 --> 00:11:08,260
can be recast as prior preferences. The organism expects to be fed, so being fed is unsurprising.

134
00:11:08,720 --> 00:11:13,880
Whether that recasting adds insight or just relabels the problem is still argued about.

135
00:11:14,740 --> 00:11:20,380
The fourth test is reward, and here the brain offers what may be the clearest machine learning

136
00:11:20,380 --> 00:11:27,440
algorithm ever found in biology. In the 1990s, Wolfram Schultz recorded dopamine neurons in

137
00:11:27,440 --> 00:11:33,100
monkeys learning that a cue predicted a squirt of juice. At first, the neurons fired a burst when

138
00:11:33,100 --> 00:11:38,560
the juice arrived. After learning, the burst moved to the cue, and the juice itself produced

139
00:11:38,560 --> 00:11:44,600
no response. And if the expected juice failed to arrive, the neurons dipped below baseline at

140
00:11:44,600 --> 00:11:50,840
exactly the moment it should have come. In 1997, Schultz, Peter Dayen, and Reed Montague pointed

141
00:11:50,840 --> 00:11:56,080
out that this is precisely the reward prediction error of temporal difference learning, an algorithm

142
00:11:56,080 --> 00:12:01,660
Richard Sutton had developed for machines. The reward you got, plus the value of where you ended

143
00:12:01,660 --> 00:12:08,220
up, minus the value you predicted. Once the cue reliably predicts juice, the juice is no longer

144
00:12:08,220 --> 00:12:14,660
news. The news arrives with the cue, and so does the burst. The same algorithm had powered TD

145
00:12:14,660 --> 00:12:21,040
Gammon, a backgammon program from the early 90s. The correspondence has deepened. In 2020,

146
00:12:21,560 --> 00:12:26,620
Will Dabney and colleagues at DeepMind and Harvard found that dopamine neurons encode not

147
00:12:26,620 --> 00:12:32,000
one expected reward, but a distribution. Some neurons are tuned to be optimistic,

148
00:12:32,520 --> 00:12:37,900
others pessimistic, and the population as a whole represents the range of possible rewards,

149
00:12:38,220 --> 00:12:44,240
exactly as in distributional reinforcement learning, which had been invented a few years earlier for AI.

150
00:12:44,760 --> 00:12:50,840
An algorithm first tested on Atari games turned out to fit dopamine recordings in mice.

151
00:12:51,240 --> 00:12:57,640
That could mean the brain runs it, or just that evolution and engineers found the same good solution.

152
00:12:57,980 --> 00:13:00,120
Either way, the match keeps growing.

153
00:13:00,630 --> 00:13:07,600
In 2025, a study in Nature reported that dopamine neurons jointly map when a reward will arrive

154
00:13:07,600 --> 00:13:14,240
and how big it will be. But even here the slogan has a challenger. In 2022, H. Wee-yung-yung,

155
00:13:14,600 --> 00:13:20,560
Vijay Nambudiri, and colleagues argued, from experiments in mice, that dopamine release looks

156
00:13:20,560 --> 00:13:26,000
more like a signal of learned causal associations, worked out by looking backward from rewards,

157
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:27,720
than a forward prediction error.

158
00:13:28,360 --> 00:13:32,580
The prediction error account remains the best-supported algorithm in biology,

159
00:13:32,580 --> 00:13:35,100
and it is still contested at the edges.

160
00:13:36,080 --> 00:13:37,920
The fifth test is maps.

161
00:13:38,220 --> 00:13:43,180
In 1948, Edward Tallman argued from experiments with rats in mazes

162
00:13:43,180 --> 00:13:45,180
that animals build cognitive maps,

163
00:13:45,440 --> 00:13:48,660
internal models that let them take shortcuts they've never tried.

164
00:13:49,060 --> 00:13:51,840
The neural evidence arrived in 1971,

165
00:13:51,840 --> 00:13:57,240
when John O'Keefe and Jonathan Dostrovsky found placed cells in the rat hippocampus,

166
00:13:57,440 --> 00:14:01,100
neurons that fire when the animal is in a particular location.

167
00:14:01,380 --> 00:14:07,120
In 2005, Maybritt and Edvard Moser's lab found grid cells in the nearby entorhinal cortex,

168
00:14:07,500 --> 00:14:12,020
which fire at many locations arranged in a strikingly regular triangular lattice,

169
00:14:12,280 --> 00:14:14,460
like coordinate lines laid over the world.

170
00:14:14,960 --> 00:14:18,500
O'Keefe and the Mosers shared the 2014 Nobel Prize.

171
00:14:19,240 --> 00:14:22,140
Nearby are head direction cells, which work like a compass,

172
00:14:22,440 --> 00:14:25,040
and boundary cells, which fire near walls.

173
00:14:25,400 --> 00:14:28,220
Together they form something close to a navigation system.

174
00:14:28,500 --> 00:14:29,780
These maps predict.

175
00:14:30,140 --> 00:14:35,040
During rest and sleep, the hippocampus replays sequences of placed cell activity,

176
00:14:35,360 --> 00:14:40,480
compressed in time, including paths the animal took and sometimes paths it has not.

177
00:14:40,660 --> 00:14:46,100
In 2017, Kimberly Stachenfeld, Matthew Botvinik, and Samuel Jerschman

178
00:14:46,100 --> 00:14:51,240
proposed that the hippocampus encodes each place by where you are likely to go next from it,

179
00:14:51,560 --> 00:14:54,880
what reinforcement learning calls a successor representation.

180
00:14:55,360 --> 00:14:59,860
That explains why place fields stretch backward along a habitual route.

181
00:15:00,020 --> 00:15:01,900
And the maps are not only spatial.

182
00:15:02,220 --> 00:15:07,640
In 2016, Alexandra Konstantinosku, Jill O'Reilly, and Timothy Behrens

183
00:15:07,640 --> 00:15:12,520
taught people an abstract space of cartoon birds whose necks and legs varied in length

184
00:15:12,520 --> 00:15:18,040
and found grid-like brain signals as people mentally moved through that conceptual space.

185
00:15:18,320 --> 00:15:23,580
The brain seems to reuse its navigation machinery to organize knowledge in general.

186
00:15:23,960 --> 00:15:30,040
James Whittington, Behrens, and colleagues then built a model called the Talman-Eichenbaum machine

187
00:15:30,040 --> 00:15:34,460
that learns to separate the structure of an environment from its sensory contents.

188
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:38,320
So the same structural knowledge carries over to new places,

189
00:15:38,320 --> 00:15:42,100
and its units developed place-like and grid-like responses.

190
00:15:42,520 --> 00:15:48,960
In 2022, Whittington, Joseph Warren, and Behrens showed that transformers with a particular kind

191
00:15:48,960 --> 00:15:54,640
of position encoding are closely related, mathematically, to that model, and reproduce

192
00:15:54,640 --> 00:16:01,060
the same cell types. A model of the hippocampus and the architecture of modern AI turned out to

193
00:16:01,060 --> 00:16:07,700
be cousins. So what survives the five tests? Perception combines priors with evidence,

194
00:16:07,700 --> 00:16:10,060
roughly in proportion to reliability.

195
00:16:10,560 --> 00:16:13,240
The cortex is full of expectation signals

196
00:16:13,240 --> 00:16:15,860
even if the textbook circuit is under dispute.

197
00:16:16,140 --> 00:16:18,820
The free energy principle is better described

198
00:16:18,820 --> 00:16:22,520
as a powerful mathematical lens than as a testable theory.

199
00:16:22,800 --> 00:16:26,060
Reward learning is the clearest case of a machine algorithm

200
00:16:26,060 --> 00:16:27,500
running in biology.

201
00:16:27,840 --> 00:16:30,440
And the hippocampus builds predictive maps

202
00:16:30,440 --> 00:16:32,260
and one leading model of them

203
00:16:32,260 --> 00:16:35,680
turns out to be a close mathematical cousin of the transformer.

204
00:16:35,680 --> 00:16:42,560
In every case, the brain is learning a model of its world and using it to anticipate what comes next.

205
00:16:43,080 --> 00:16:45,700
And all of it could happen without anyone home.

206
00:16:46,140 --> 00:16:48,980
Nothing in this episode needed the word experience.

207
00:16:49,500 --> 00:16:51,280
That's the point to hold on to.

208
00:16:51,660 --> 00:16:57,560
Some theories we'll meet in episode 11 argue that experience is made of exactly this kind of prediction,

209
00:16:57,880 --> 00:17:00,620
especially prediction about keeping a body alive.

210
00:17:01,100 --> 00:17:03,500
Others say prediction is beside the point.

211
00:17:03,500 --> 00:17:08,940
But first, the machines, how they learn, and what researchers have found inside them.

